olic rather than a jew  but still styron caught a fair amount of  criticism for those  uh  uh  experiments too  i think he will be remembered for many  things  as a writer but maybe you have hit on  the most  important one that is his willingness to  cross boundaries  to cross boundaries of race or ethnicity  or experience  and to try to embody  those big tough  questions of our century  in his novels  okay  um you opened by describing star as a  southern writer and then qualifying a  little bit by talking about newport news  as not being old south  i'm curious as to  what makes him still be considered a  southern writer he hasn't really lived  in the south for i guess for 50 years  and except for lie down in darkness  which clearly comes out of a southern  tradition the other novels don't  seem to be as much the works of a  southern writer yet he's referred to  that way so is there something about his  writing that  makes that or is it just the  happenstance of his birth  i think actually it's undoubtedly my own  prejudice in this manner  now um  you're right uh he is a southern writer  who has moved out and beyond his his  southern roots  uh i think the influences that have  worked on saren have been  at least as much cosmopolitan  particularly his periods of residence in  france and in italy  and his experience in europe generally  i think styron understood early in his  career that  if he stuck with  exclusively with southern material he  ran the risk of being seen as a  parochial writer  a regionalist and he really isn't  so you're right  i do think though that  the experience of his first  oh  15 years particularly in newport news  and then his education which was in  southern universities  has had strong influence on him and he  returns to those subjects even in  sophie's choice and set this house on  fire which have uh long parts of them  that are set in the south and then of  course the confessions of matt turner  and lydon and darkness are both uh set  in 